id: k8s.pvc
kind: kubernetes
title: PersistentVolumeClaim
aliases: pvc, persistent volume claim
tags: storage, state
questions:
  - how does a pod get durable storage?
invariant: A PVC is a request for storage that binds to a PersistentVolume (statically or dynamically) and survives Pod rescheduling.
answer_30s: A PVC declares size and access mode; a StorageClass provisions a PV for it, and the Pod mounts the claim, not the disk.
answer_2m: |
  Two controllers are involved: dynamic provisioning creates the PV when the
  claim appears, and attachment only happens when a Pod that uses the claim is
  scheduled onto a node -- which is why a ReadWriteOnce volume pins a Pod to one
  node at a time. Reclaim policy and StatefulSet ownership are the follow-ups:
  deleting a StatefulSet does not delete its PVCs, which is deliberate, and
  scaling down does not either.
when_to_use:
  - databases, uploads, anything that must survive a Pod
avoid_when:
  - caches and derived data (a volume is a liability you must back up)
commands:
  - kubectl get pvc -A => which claims exist and their binding status
  - kubectl describe pvc data-db-0 => why a claim is Pending (no storage class, no capacity, access mode mismatch)
  - kubectl get pv => which backing volumes are bound or Released
failure_modes:
  - claim Pending because no default StorageClass exists
  - ReadWriteOnce expected to be shared between nodes
  - node lost while a ReadWriteMany-less volume was attached, delaying rescheduling
related:
  - k8s.storage-class
  - k8s.stateful-set
  - k8s.volume
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/
